course: dictionary
lesson: 4223
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the words around them tell you which meaning is meant. Graft is a good example. Read the clues before you choose.
explain: «so orchards grow the same kind by grafting, joining a twig of a good tree onto young roots.» The clues are twig, tree and roots, so graft means joining one plant onto another so they grow together.
explain: In Britain, a friend who says «hard graft» is praising your effort, not talking about plants or skin. In US news, graft means officials using their power dishonestly to get money. Same word, three jobs.
explain: Graft can also be a verb, so you may see grafted or grafting. Try your meaning in the sentence. If joining plants or tissue fits, it is the plant or body sense. If working hard fits, it is the everyday sense.
fact: Graft can be a noun (a thing) or a verb (an action).
fact: A graft can be a piece of skin or bone moved by a surgeon to mend another part of the body.
fact: A graft can also be a cut piece of plant joined onto another plant so they grow together.
fact: In Britain and Australia, informal graft means hard work, so «hard graft» praises someone's effort.
fact: In US news, graft means dishonest use of power to get money, like a bribe. Most fruit trees are grafted, so a tasty branch can grow on a strong root.
exercise: «so orchards grow the same kind by grafting, joining a twig of a good tree onto young roots.» In this sentence, what is grafting? | Washing apples in water | *Joining a twig of a good tree onto young roots | Digging up roots to throw away | Counting how many apples fall
exercise: In Britain, a friend says your «hard graft» on the project was brilliant. What does graft mean here? | Dishonest use of power for money | Skin moved by a surgeon | *Hard work | A cut piece of plant
exercise: A US news report says officials used graft to get money from builders. What does graft mean? | A plant joined onto another plant | Skin moved to heal a burn | *Dishonest use of power to get money | Hard work on a building site
test: A surgeon uses a skin graft to repair a burn. What is the graft here? | A cut twig joined to a plant | Dishonest use of power | *A piece of body tissue moved to repair another part | Hard work
test: Which word is a verb in this sentence? «She will graft the pear branch onto a strong root tomorrow.» | pear | root | *graft | branch
exam: In British English, which meaning fits «He had to graft all weekend to finish the fence»? | Moved skin to repair a burn | Used power dishonestly for money | *Worked very hard | Joined a plant onto another
